meeting of the Youth & Family Action Committee was called to order by Council\n  Member Rick Crosby at 5:04 p.m.\n\n  Executive Board Members present:\n\n  Executive Board\n  Rick Crosby, Council Member\n  Wendy Lau, Council Member\n  Greg Palatto, BUSD\n  Denise Fisher, Resident\n  Don Kendrick, Resident\n\n2. Review of Minutes  Minutes for the August 27, 2024, meeting were approved.\n\n3. SRO Report  Student Resource Officer (SRO) Burgos has returned\n  from paternity leave. He said LVPD is working with\n  parents about traffic laws, red zones, double parking,\n  crosswalks and such throughout the city’s schools. The\n  school year has started off well. LVPD is working to show\n  as much presence as possible to help the students and\n  parents know that the kids are safe while at school.\n\n4. BUSD Report  BUSD Representative Steven Patterson shared that\n  there are four new principals in BUSD this year and they\n  are doing a fabulous job in their new roles. The school\n  year has started with staff/faculty hitting the ground\n  running. The district is reviewing policies and procedures,\n  focusing on training, and revamping administrative\n  processes. The attendance incentive is expected to be\n  rolled out soon. The schools are seeing a reduction in\n  discipline and helping students continue the trend of\n  positive performance.\n\n5. Teach & Transform Grant  Mary Udria and Cherry Agapito of BUSD presented their\n  grant-funded project, highlighting the development of the\n  Roynon Sensory Assistive Technology Library, which\n  they initiated approximately 10 years ago. Understanding\n  that learning is not uniform for all students, they have\n  expanded their program by incorporating various\n  assistive devices. These include a phonetic spelling\n  helper, a Boogie Board reusable writing tablet, an ABC\n  order keyboard for students with scanning difficulties, and\n  a large, colorful keyboard designed for visually impaired\n  students. They also demonstrated a pen reader capable\n\fYouth & Family Action Committee Minutes September 24, 2024, page 2\n\n  of scanning and reading text aloud in multiple languages,\n  including English, Mandarin, and other options, with\n  functionality on both physical and digital media.\n\n6. Cell Phone & Social Media Use Education Night\n\n  Assistant City Manager JR Ranells discussed an idea\n  proposed by Carolyn Cockrell to organize an educational\n  night and raised the question of whether it should be a\n  YFAC initiative or a district-led event. JR noted that YFAC\n  had attempted a similar event in the past with limited\n  turnout. Council Member Crosby added that the district\n  had also experienced low attendance with similar efforts.\n  The topic remains important but requires strategic\n  planning. One suggestion was to pilot the event at\n  Roynon, possibly aligning it with an existing PTA meeting\n  to attract parents who are already present.\n  Communication would be facilitated through Parent\n  Square and social media, with the event tentatively\n  planned for mid-school year, following the holiday break.\n\n7. Public Comment  Community Risk Reduction Specialist Lonnie Shipman\n  shared about preparedness and how the La Verne Fire\n  Department partners with the school district to do tours.\n  He also shared that they will do presentations at the\n  schools to discuss fire safety and the explore program, or\n  focus on specific topics that the school is interested in.\n  CRRS Shipman also spoke about LVFD’s CERT program\n  and Fire Prevention Week being the first week in October.\n  Greg Palatto shared that the Summer Lunch Program\n  which will now be carried out by the school district will still\n  be located at Las Flores Park. He also noted that it would\n  be Fentanyl Awareness Week the next week, and Council\n  Member Crosby noted that LA County libraries are giving\n  out free Narcan with the closest one to La Verne being in\n  Claremont.\n  Council Member Lau talked about how it is Suicide\n  Prevention Month and wanted the committee to share\n  Tri-City Mental Health resources with as many people as\n  possible. She shared that Tri-City Mental Health is both\n  able to help with prevention and treatment when it comes\n  to such topics.\n  Council Member Crosby reminded the committee that the\n  Halloween Hop dance for special needs would be coming\n  up in October with the city’s recreation department.\n\fYouth & Family Action Committee Minutes September 24, 2024, page 3\n\n10. Next Meeting  The next YFAC meeting was scheduled for October 29,\n  2024 at 5:00pm.\n\n11. Adjournment  The meeting was adjourned at 5:47 p.m.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\n\nErin Beilstein\nSenior Management Analyst"}